 "action_summary": "The owner chose, on 2026-11-11, to have refactoring actions taught to the front door as programs run in the sandbox. Before teaching, 21 plain refactoring sentences were asked through the front door on main. Three of the four actions the owner named already exist as rows (rename a symbol, extract a function, move a function). What was wrong was how sentences were read, and three readings answered PASS while doing something else: (1) 'rename the variable tmp to product in the function compute' renamed the FUNCTION, because the object-role rule refused a row only when it shared no role with the sentence and this row shared 'function'; quoted names stated no role at all; (2) 'extract ... lines 13 to 14' extracted line 13, and so did 'lines 13 to 16', because on that lane nothing read the numbers and a search for the smallest self-contained block happened to start on the stated line; (3) '... into a new function scale' produced a function called _extracted_helper. This change makes the role rule require that a parameter or variable the sentence names be something the row can hold, reads quoted names, binds stated line numbers as written and derives the enclosing function from them when none is named, and uses the placeholder name only when the sentence names nothing. It then teaches the two actions that did not exist, each as a self-contained program checked by the existing demonstration-to-skill compiler on a second example: rename a local variable, and inline a local variable that is set once and read once in the next statement as the first thing that statement computes. Each program verifies its own edit against the parse tree before writing and refuses otherwise. The compiled skills are exported to a tracked bundle; a skill database that already exists now receives skills added to the bundle later; a taught action that declines a case reports its reason through the door.",
